@gs_ai_ introduces Genesis World 1.0, a new simulation platform for robotics and embodied AI that is open-sourced. Robotics development is limited by the slow, expensive process of testing on physical hardware, with one hour in real life equating to 100 days in simulation. To address this, Genesis World 1.0 offers near real-time performance through GPU-accelerated simulation, penetration-free contact physics, and support for deformable objects, enabling faster and more reliable model iteration.

The platform includes Nyx, a high-performance path-traced renderer, and is built on Quadrants, a GPU-optimized cross-platform compiler that improves simulation speed by up to 4.6 times. These advances reduce the gap between simulation and reality, allowing for zero-shot real-to-sim evaluation and accelerating robotics research. The tools are available today, with links to the GitHub repositories for Genesis World, Quadrants, and Nyx.
